l":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815395","date_published":"2026-04-18T12:29:06Z","author":{"name":"RickJWagner","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=RickJWagner"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815127","title":"The quiet disappearance of the free-range childhood","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://bigthink.com/mind-behavior/the-quiet-disappearance-of-the-free-range-childhood/\"\u003ehttps://bigthink.com/mind-behavior/the-quiet-disappearance-of-the-free-range-childhood/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815127\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815127\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 184\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 190\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://bigthink.com/mind-behavior/the-quiet-disappearance-of-the-free-range-childhood/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815127","date_published":"2026-04-18T11:43:57Z","author":{"name":"sylvainkalache","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=sylvainkalache"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815118","title":"State of Kdenlive","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://kdenlive.org/news/2026/state-2026/\"\u003ehttps://kdenlive.org/news/2026/state-2026/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815118\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815118\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 324\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 108\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://kdenlive.org/news/2026/state-2026/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47815118","date_published":"2026-04-18T11:42:37Z","author":{"name":"f_r_d","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=f_r_d"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813668","title":"Category Theory Illustrated – Orders","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://abuseofnotation.github.io/category-theory-illustrated/04_order/\"\u003ehttps://abuseofnotation.github.io/category-theory-illustrated/04_order/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813668\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813668\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 219\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 58\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://abuseofnotation.github.io/category-theory-illustrated/04_order/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813668","date_published":"2026-04-18T06:40:47Z","author":{"name":"boris_m","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=boris_m"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813566","title":"Amiga Graphics Archive","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://amiga.lychesis.net/\"\u003ehttps://amiga.lychesis.net/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813566\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813566\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 227\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 69\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://amiga.lychesis.net/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47813566","date_published":"2026-04-18T06:20:04Z","author":{"name":"sph","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=sph"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47812341","title":"Show HN: I made a calculator that works over disjoint sets of intervals","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eI've been studying interval arithmetic for the past few weeks and it's a really interesting field because while there is a ton of super interesting research published over the past decades, it has never really gotten the recognition that it deserves, IMO.\u003cp\u003eOne reason for this is that standard interval arithmetic has really poor handling of division by intervals containing zero. If you compute 1 / [-1, 2] in regular interval arithmetic, you get either [-∞, +∞], or you have to say that the operation is undefined. Both solutions are virtually useless. The real answer of course is [-∞, -1] U [0.5, +∞]: i.e. a union of two disjoint intervals.\u003cp\u003eThis is useful because you can confidently exclude a non empty set of the real numbers ([-1, 0.5]) from the set of possible values that you can get by dividing 1 by a number between -1 and 2.\u003cp\u003eBut this definition of interval division yields a value that is not an interval. This is a problem if you want to define a closed arithmetic system, where you can build and evaluate arbitrary expression over interval values.\u003cp\u003e(This behavior extends to any non continuous function like tan() for example, which is implemented in my project - not without difficulties!)\u003cp\u003eWell the obvious solution is to define your arithmetic over disjoint unions of intervals. This is the subject of a 2017 paper called \"Interval Unions\" by by Schichl, H., Domes, F., Montanher, T. and Kofler, K..\u003cp\u003eThis open-source project I made implements interval union arithmetic in TypeScript in the form of a simple interactive calculator, so you can try it out for yourself! The underlying TypeScript library is dependency free and implements interval union arithmetic over IEEE 754 double precision floats (JS native number type) with outward rounding. This guarantees accuracy of interval results in the presence of rounding issue inherent to floating point.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47812341\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47812341\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 288\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 49\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://victorpoughon.github.io/interval-calculator/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47812341","date_published":"2026-04-18T01:15:43Z","author":{"name":"fouronnes3","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=fouronnes3"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47811283","title":"Landmark ancient-genome study shows surprise acceleration of human evolution","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eRelated: \u003ci\u003eAncient DNA reveals pervasive directional selection across West Eurasia [pdf]\u003c/i\u003e - \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47791282\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47791282\u003c/a\u003e (64 comments)\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://x.com/doctorveera/status/2044679999450664967\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003ehttps://x.com/doctorveera/status/2044679999450664967\u003c/a\u003e (\u003ca href=\"https://xcancel.com/doctorveera/status/2044679999450664967\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003ehttps://xcancel.com/doctorveera/status/2044679999450664967\u003c/a\u003e)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47811283\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47811283\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 106\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 126\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-01204-5","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47811283","date_published":"2026-04-17T22:30:28Z","author":{"name":"unsuspecting","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=unsuspecting"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47810872","title":"A simplified model of Fil-C","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://www.corsix.org/content/simplified-model-of-fil-c\"\u003ehttps://www.corsix.org/content/simplified-model-of-fil-c\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47810872\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47810872\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 200\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 106\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://www.corsix.org/content/simplif